  "text": "Light reflected from the surfaces of many leaf types was measured with a direct‑reading light‑measuring device, positioning the leaf so the detector faced it perpendicularly while the illumination came from a surrounding sphere, hitting the leaf from many angles. The reflected fraction changed with wavelength, peaking in the green region (around the middle of the visible spectrum). Dark green leaves reflected roughly 6–8 % at this peak, whereas lighter green leaves reflected up to about 20–25 %. The presence of surface hairs or a glossy cuticle did not consistently increase reflectance; some hairy leaves showed only a slight rise, while in a few cases hairs on the underside boosted reflectance. As leaves aged, reflectance fell, coinciding with rapid early chlorophyll buildup that later plateaued until chlorophyll began to break down in late summer. Pure white surfaces reflected uniformly across wavelengths, while albino leaves reflected mainly longer wavelengths and absorbed blue light, giving about 40–50 % overall reflectance. Surface mold or similar whitish growths could more than double reflectance. Development of red pigments shifted the reflectance peak toward longer wavelengths, and yellow autumn coloration produced higher reflectance (around 42 % with a peak near the red end of the spectrum) compared with red tones."
